{"assets":["/editorial-assets/ecd660704b4b22843a50d13b1b2e29d918638f789140ab7d29a896919f1cf66c.png","/editorial-assets/3e8f820712b4daaefcae04005527e6b2626dd8b0b3a3eb4239c419a2da70c250.png","/editorial-assets/1840bdc523bba48106f006743b4c0238149c1e235a1cd093dbc4feb76f779476.png","/editorial-assets/867ae964f2de2d11dc44b01abf49c03dd328104ae8e8583356d3d1ea2b089f11.png"],"contestId":"17","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/447"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Contest discussion"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"children":[{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"Problem A. Noldbach problem"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"}],"kind":"strong"},{"kind":"text","text":"To solve this problem you were to find prime numbers in range [2.."},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"N"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]. The constraints were pretty small, so you could do that in any way - using the Sieve of Eratosthenes or simply looping over all possible divisors of a number."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"Take every pair of neighboring prime numbers and check if their sum increased by 1 is a prime number too. Count the number of these pairs, compare it to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"K"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and output the result."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem B. Hierarchy"}],"kind":"strong"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"Note that if employee, except one, has exactly one supervisor, then our hierarchy will be tree-like for sure."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"For each employee consider all applications in which he appears as a subordinate. If for more than one employee there are no such applications at all, it's obvious that  - 1 is the answer. In other case, for each employee find such an application with minimal cost and add these costs to get the answer."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"Alternatively, you could use Kruskal's algorithm finding a minimum spanning tree for a graph. But you should be careful so that you don't assign the second supervisor to some employee."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"And yes, the employees' qualifications weren't actually needed to solve this problem :)"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Problem C. Balance"},{"kind":"line_break"}],"kind":"strong"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"Consider the input string "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" of length "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Let's perform some operations from the problem statement on this string; suppose we obtained some string "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Let "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"compression"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" of string "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"X"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" be a string "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"X"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"' obtained from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"X"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" by replacing all consecutive equal letters with one such letter. For example, if "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"S"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = \""},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"aabcccbbaa"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"\", then its compression "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"S"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"' = \""},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"abcba"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"\"."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"Now, consider compressions of strings "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"' and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"'. It can be proven that if "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" can be obtained from some string "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" using some number of operations from the problem statement, then "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"' is a subsequence of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"', and vice versa - if for any strings "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" of equal length "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"' is a subsequence of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"', then string "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" can be obtained from string "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" using some number of operations."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"Intuitively you can understand it in this manner: suppose we use some letter "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" from position "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"A"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in order to put letter "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" at positions